General elections were held in [[Malawi]] on 20 May 2014. They were Malawi's first tripartite elections, the first time the president, National Assembly and local councillors were elected on the same day.<ref name="MEC">"[http://www.mec.org.mw/News/TripatiteElections/tabid/110/Default.aspx Malawi Election Commission News] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140201233745/http://www.mec.org.mw/News/TripatiteElections/tabid/110/Default.aspx |date=1 February 2014 }}", 2009</ref> The presidential election was won by opposition candidate [[Peter Mutharika]] of the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]], who defeated incumbent President [[Joyce Banda]].

==Background==
Malawi's [[Consumer price index|CPI]] increased by 27.7% in 2013, but its [[Gross domestic product|GDP]] grew by only 5%.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/weo/2014/01/weodata/weorept.aspx?pr.x=49&pr.y=13&sy=2013&ey=2013&scsm=1&ssd=1&sort=country&ds=.&br=1&c=676&s=NGDP_RPCH%2CPCPIPCH&grp=0&a=|title = World Economic Outlook Database|publisher = International Monetary Fund|date = April 2014}}</ref> Malawi has maintained a [[Polity data series|polity score]] of 6 since 2005, designating it as a [[democracy]].<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.systemicpeace.org/polity/maw2.htm|title = Authority Trends, 1964–2010: Malawi|accessdate = 5 May 2014|website = Polity IV Project}}</ref> Malawi's previous [[2009 Malawian general election|presidential election in 2009]] was only the fourth such election in the country's history following the end of [[Hastings Banda]]'s period of one-man rule in 1994.

[[Bingu wa Mutharika]], who had won the [[2004 Malawian general election|2004 election]], picked his foreign minister [[Joyce Banda]] to be vice president after successfully contesting the [[2009 Malawian general election|2009 election]]. She was expelled from the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]] and formed the [[People's Party (Malawi)|People's Party]] when she refused to endorse President Bingu wa Mutharika's younger brother [[Peter Mutharika]] for president in the 2014 general election.<ref name=guardian>[https://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/may/24/madonna-malawi-president-joyce-banda-elections Madonna attacks Malawi's president after attempt to nullify elections] ''The Guardian''</ref>

Bingu wa Mutharika died of a heart attack on 5 April 2012, and Joyce Banda took the presidency two days later, in accordance with the constitution, despite protests from some ruling party officials that Banda's expulsion from the ruling party made her ineligible.<ref>Kim Yi Dionne and Boniface Dulani. (1 January 2013) [https://academic.oup.com/afraf/article/112/446/111/10235 Constitutional provisions and executive succession: Malawi's 2012 transition in comparative perspective]. ''African Affairs''. Retrieved 14 March 2019.</ref>

The People's Party entered the election with a sitting president, but little in the way of a grass-roots machine.<ref name=guardian/>

