==Results==
Amid a breakdown in electronic systems for relaying results back to IEC headquarters, Banda claimed fraud and attempted to cancel the election after only one third of the votes were counted, and [[Peter Mutharika]], brother of the president who died in 2012, was well ahead. Banda said another vote should be held within 90 days, and she said she would not be standing, but opposition parties and the [[Malawi Law Society]] objected.<ref>[http://www.news24.com/Africa/News/Malawi-to-recount-votes-after-fraud-claims-20140525 Malawi to recount votes after fraud claims] ''News24''</ref>

Court action ensued,<ref>[http://www.enca.com/africa/malawi-court-says-election-results-must-be-announced-recount Malawi court demands election results before recount]</ref> but the IEC said the first count would be completed, and a recount started. The result would only be announced after the recount, estimated to take two months. On 30 May 2014, the High Court ruled that any recount must be done within eight days of the vote; since the allowed period had already ended, the court ruled that the electoral commission should announce the results.<ref>Zawadi Chilunga, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/court-rules-mec-should-declare-winner-of-malawi-presidential-polls/ "Court rules MEC should declare winner of Malawi presidential polls"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140531090358/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/court-rules-mec-should-declare-winner-of-malawi-presidential-polls/ |date=31 May 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 30 May 2014.</ref> Accordingly, the electoral commission declared later in the day that Mutharika had won the presidential election with 36.4% of the vote. Another opposition candidate, [[Lazarus Chakwera]] of the MCP, received 27.8%, while Banda trailed in third place with 20.2%. The head of the commission, [[Maxon Mbendera]], said that "the rule of law compels us to release the results", although he acknowledged that some of the commission's members had "reservations" about them.<ref>Zawadi Chilunga, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/mutharika-declared-winner-in-malawi-2014-presidential-election/ "Mutharika declared winner in Malawi 2014 presidential election"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140531090303/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/mutharika-declared-winner-in-malawi-2014-presidential-election/ |date=31 May 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 30 May 2014.</ref> Banda quickly said that she accepted the outcome, although she continued to describe the vote as "fraudulent".<ref>Wanga Gwede, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/jb-accepts-to-hand-over-power-to-apm-malawi-presidency/ "JB accepts to hand over power to APM: Malawi presidency"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140531090402/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/30/jb-accepts-to-hand-over-power-to-apm-malawi-presidency/ |date=31 May 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 30 May 2014.</ref>

Mutharika was sworn in as president on the morning of 31 May 2014.<ref>Zawadi Chilunga, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/31/so-help-me-god-mutharika-sworn-in-as-malawi-president-chilima-vp/ "'So help me God': Mutharika sworn in as Malawi President, Chilima VP"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140601004431/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/31/so-help-me-god-mutharika-sworn-in-as-malawi-president-chilima-vp/ |date=1 June 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 31 May 2014.</ref> Banda congratulated Mutharika and called for national unity, urging the people to support the new president and wishing him success.<ref>Thom Chiumia, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/31/defeated-jb-congratulates-mutharika-malawi-must-move-forward/ "Defeated JB congratulates Mutharika: 'Malawi must move forward'"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140601004412/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/05/31/defeated-jb-congratulates-mutharika-malawi-must-move-forward/ |date=1 June 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 31 May 2014.</ref> An inauguration ceremony for Mutharika was held in [[Blantyre]] on 2 June 2014. Banda was not present at the inauguration, held at [[Kamuzu Stadium]], and her absence was viewed by some as a snub, given the history of bitter rivalry between the two. Mutharika said that he was offering an [[olive branch]] and wanted to "bury the past", expressing "regret" at Banda's absence. He said that he was not interested in "vengeance", although he added that "those who have broken the law will face the full course of justice".<ref>Zawadi Chilunga, [http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/06/02/mutharika-regrets-jbs-snub-in-power-handover-muluzi-cheered/ "Mutharika regrets JB's snub in power handover: Muluzi cheered"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140606212425/http://www.nyasatimes.com/2014/06/02/mutharika-regrets-jbs-snub-in-power-handover-muluzi-cheered/ |date=6 June 2014 }}, ''Nyasa Times'', 2 June 2014.</ref>

